    "benefit": "In the original100% oblique/macro and75% oblique views, the large hard bright wedge or thin vertical sheet-like skin pull immediately outside/below the commissures is visibly reduced. The transition spreads into surrounding skin more gradually. This is a perceptible local benefit absent from candidate01.",
    "preservedAppearance": "The central lip body, mouth width and main contact line appear retained in these six images. Contact records provide separate technical evidence of original sampled trajectories.",
    "tradeoffsAndRemainingDefects": "The pointed commissure remains. Horizontal/fan-like fold bands around the outer corners are more conspicuous in parts of the high-progress result. The inner lip edge still looks thin and the whole corner is not convincingly natural.",
    "newOuterBoundary": "No single new hard distant support cutoff was identified in the six original images; this does not certify all transition geometry. The more salient upper/outer corner folds are a real visual concern, not dismissed because the matrix converged.",
    "scope": "Three matched pairs personally inspected at original640x512;25%/50% are numerically reviewed from QA, not claimed as personally viewed in this review."
  },
  "teamDisposition": {
    "rootDecision": "reject-current-candidate-for-delivery",
    "independentVisualReviewerDecision": "reject-current-candidate",
    "agreedObservation": "The previous large bright wedge is reduced; the corner tip remains.",
    "disagreement": "This geometry reviewer initially judged the local gain worth retaining under a narrow claim, while root and the independent visual reviewer judged the upper/outer stepped bands, left angular dark hollow and right stacked ridges at75/100% to outweigh that gain. This record preserves that independent interpretation rather than revising it to match consensus.",
    "action": "Do not deliver candidate02 as accepted improvement. Preserve its source and evidence and use the observed gain/regression to formulate the next falsifiable candidate. A dark hollow in the image is not asserted to be a geometric hole."
  },
